      <h1>Wolverhampton Taxi Test: Questions and Answers</h1>

      <p>
        If you're preparing for the Wolverhampton private hire knowledge
        test, this page gives you real exam-style
        <strong>questions and answers</strong> to practise with, plus a clear
        breakdown of what the test actually covers. It's free, and it's
        written specifically for the Wolverhampton PHV test — not a generic
        UK taxi quiz.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Wolverhampton PHV knowledge test is the licensing exam every
        driver must pass to get their taxi badge under Wolverhampton City
        Council's framework. It has a reputation for being harder than people
        expect — and the numbers back that up. You need to score at least
        <strong>75% (21 out of 28 questions)</strong>, but there's a second,
        stricter rule that catches many drivers out: the eight key questions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Get a single key question wrong, and you fail the entire paper — no
        matter how well you did on everything else. That's why so many
        well-prepared drivers still fail on their first attempt, and why
        resitters make up a large share of the people using this page. If
        you've failed before, you're in the right place.
      </p>

      <h2>What the Wolverhampton taxi test covers</h2>

      <p>
        The test draws from an official syllabus of around 25 topics, which
        group into seven core categories. Here's what each one tests and why
        it matters.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Safeguarding.</strong> Protecting children and vulnerable
          adults from abuse, exploitation, and neglect. This is the
          highest-stakes category on the test — drivers are often the eyes
          and ears of their community, and the council expects you to
          recognise warning signs and know how to report concerns. You are
          expected to report, not investigate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plying for Hire.</strong> The legal line between a private
          hire vehicle and a Hackney Carriage. PHVs must always be pre-booked
          through a licensed operator — you cannot pick up people who flag
          you down or wait on a taxi rank. Getting this wrong isn't just an
          exam fail; in real life it invalidates your insurance and can cost
          you your licence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Disability Awareness.</strong> Your duties under the
          Equality Act 2010, including assistance dogs, reasonable
          adjustments, and how to treat disabled passengers with dignity.
          Refusing an assistance dog or charging extra for one is unlawful
          discrimination.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Customer Care and Safety.</strong> How to treat passengers
          professionally and keep everyone safe — reasonable assistance with
          luggage and seatbelts, handling difficult situations, and
          protecting yourself against false allegations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Licensing and Compliance.</strong> The rules that keep your
          licence valid — insurance, vehicle checks, reporting convictions,
          dashcam and CCTV registration, and the conditions attached to your
          badge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vehicle and Road Safety.</strong> Keeping your vehicle
          roadworthy and safe, carrying out walkaround checks, and meeting
          Wolverhampton's vehicle standards.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Law and Regulations.</strong> The legislation behind the
          job, chiefly the Local Government (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act
          1976, which underpins how private hire licensing works.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        For a full breakdown of how to revise each area, see our guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass/"
          >how to pass the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>The 8 key questions — and why they matter most</h2>

      <p>
        This is the single most important thing to understand about the test.
      </p>

      <p>
        Within the 28 questions, eight are designated
        <strong>key questions</strong>. Unlike the rest, these are
        pass-or-fail on their own: get even one wrong and you fail the whole
        paper automatically, regardless of your overall score. You could
        answer 27 out of 28 correctly and still fail if the one you missed
        was a key question.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key questions are concentrated in the highest-stakes categories.
        Based on the official test structure, they break down as
        <strong>three from Safeguarding, three from Plying for Hire, and two
        from Disability Awareness</strong>. That's not a coincidence — these
        are the areas where a wrong answer in real life could put a
        vulnerable person at risk, break the law, or invalidate your
        insurance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here's the trap: most drivers revise broadly, spreading their effort
        evenly across all seven categories. But because the key questions
        carry so much more weight, a driver who knows the general material
        well can still fail on a single safeguarding or plying-for-hire
        question they never drilled properly. Broad revision isn't enough —
        you have to drill the key question areas specifically until the
        correct answers are automatic.
      </p>

      <p>
        We cover exactly how these work in our dedicated guide to
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/8-key-questions/">the 8 key questions</a>.
        The PHV Prep UK app also includes a focused key questions drill that
        targets these areas directly.
      </p>

      <h2>Sample questions and answers</h2>

      <p>
        Here are ten Wolverhampton-specific practice questions with answers
        and explanations. These are written in the style and difficulty of
        the real test, grounded in UK law and council policy. At least two
        come from the highest-stakes safeguarding and disability awareness
        categories.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 1 — Plying for Hire</h3>

      <p>
        A member of the public flags you down in the street and asks for a
        lift. You are a licensed private hire driver. What should you do?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Take them if they agree to pay</li>
        <li>B. Take them, then log it as a booking afterwards</li>
        <li>C. Refuse, give them the operator's number, and move on</li>
        <li>D. Take them only if it's a short journey</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> A private hire vehicle may only
        carry passengers whose journey has been pre-booked through a licensed
        operator. Accepting a flag-down is illegal plying for hire — and
        creating the booking after the fact does not make it legal. Only
        Hackney Carriages may accept street hails.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 2 — Plying for Hire</h3>

      <p>
        If a PHV driver picks up a passenger who has not booked through the
        operator, what happens to the insurance for that journey?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. It remains fully valid</li>
        <li>B. It is invalidated completely</li>
        <li>C. It provides third-party cover only</li>
        <li>D. It is valid for journeys under five miles</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: B.</strong> Private hire insurance only
        covers journeys pre-booked through the licensed operator. An unbooked
        pickup invalidates the policy entirely, leaving the driver personally
        liable for any injury or damage. This is one reason illegal plying is
        treated so seriously.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 3 — Safeguarding</h3>

      <p>You suspect a passenger is being abused. What is your responsibility?</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Gather evidence before saying anything</li>
        <li>
          B. Report your concerns to the authorities — investigating is not
          your role
        </li>
        <li>C. Confront the suspected abuser directly</li>
        <li>D. Do nothing unless the passenger asks for help</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: B.</strong> Drivers report concerns to
        safeguarding agencies such as the police or the council's
        safeguarding team. Investigation is the job of professional services.
        Trying to investigate yourself can put you and the victim at risk and
        may compromise a later inquiry.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 4 — Safeguarding</h3>

      <p>
        Is it acceptable to give a vulnerable passenger your personal mobile
        number?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Yes, if they ask for it</li>
        <li>B. Yes, for regular passengers</li>
        <li>C. No</li>
        <li>D. Only in an emergency</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> Giving a vulnerable passenger
        your personal number blurs professional boundaries and creates a
        grooming risk. All contact should go through your operator. This is
        exactly the kind of judgement a key question is designed to test.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 5 — Disability Awareness</h3>

      <p>
        A passenger books a journey and has a guide dog. You are afraid of
        dogs. Can you refuse to carry the dog?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Yes — it's your vehicle</li>
        <li>
          B. No — only a council-issued medical exemption certificate
          justifies refusal
        </li>
        <li>C. Yes, if the passenger agrees</li>
        <li>D. Yes, once per shift</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: B.</strong> Under the Equality Act 2010,
        fear, dislike, or religious objection are not lawful grounds to
        refuse an assistance dog. The only defence is a medical exemption
        certificate issued by the council, for example for a severe allergy.
        Refusing without one is unlawful disability discrimination.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 6 — Disability Awareness</h3>

      <p>
        Can you charge a passenger extra for carrying their assistance dog?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Yes, a small cleaning fee</li>
        <li>B. Yes, if the journey is long</li>
        <li>C. No — the dog must be carried free of charge</li>
        <li>D. Only if the dog sheds</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> Charging any additional fee for
        an assistance dog — including a cleaning charge — is unlawful
        discrimination under the Equality Act 2010. The dog must be carried
        free of any extra charge.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 7 — Plying for Hire</h3>

      <p>
        Is a private hire driver allowed to wait on a Hackney Carriage taxi
        rank?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Yes, if there is space</li>
        <li>B. Yes, at night only</li>
        <li>C. No, never</li>
        <li>D. Only to drop off a passenger</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> Taxi ranks are reserved
        exclusively for Hackney Carriages licensed by Wolverhampton. Under
        the city centre Traffic Regulation Order, a private hire vehicle may
        not enter a rank at all — not even to drop off or pick up. Council
        cameras monitor this, and enforcement action follows.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 8 — Customer Care and Safety</h3>

      <p>
        An elderly passenger asks you to carry their shopping into their
        house. What should you do?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Refuse and leave the bags in the vehicle</li>
        <li>
          B. Politely take the shopping to their front door, but not inside
        </li>
        <li>C. Take everything inside and put it away</li>
        <li>D. Charge extra to take it inside</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: B.</strong> Reasonable assistance includes
        carrying luggage or shopping to the door. Going inside a passenger's
        home creates safeguarding risks and exposes you to false allegations.
        Help to the door, then stop there.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 9 — Safeguarding</h3>

      <p>
        Are children with disabilities more or less likely to be abused than
        other children?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. Less likely</li>
        <li>B. Equally likely</li>
        <li>C. More likely</li>
        <li>D. They are never abused</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> Research consistently shows
        children with disabilities are at significantly higher risk of all
        forms of abuse, because of communication barriers, greater dependence
        on others, and social isolation. Awareness of this raised risk is
        part of your safeguarding duty.
      </p>

      <h3>Question 10 — Licensing and Compliance</h3>

      <p>
        Your licence has been suspended. What does this mean for your work?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A. You can work as normal</li>
        <li>B. You can work weekends only</li>
        <li>C. You cannot work at all</li>
        <li>D. You can work with a temporary badge</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong>Correct answer: C.</strong> A suspended licence means you
        must stop work immediately as a private hire driver, and cannot drive
        again until the suspension is lifted. Continuing to work while
        suspended is a serious offence.
      </p>

      <h2>How to prepare for the test</h2>

      <p>
        Practising a handful of questions is a good start, but passing the
        real test takes a focused approach. Here's what works.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Start with the eight key questions, not the full
          bank.</strong> Because one wrong key answer fails the whole paper,
          this is where your revision has the highest return. Drill
          Safeguarding, Plying for Hire, and Disability Awareness until the
          correct answers are second nature.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do timed mock exams.</strong> The real test is 28 questions
          under time pressure. Practising full mocks in the same format
          builds the stamina and pace you need, and shows you where you're
          weak before it counts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Retry the questions you get wrong.</strong> Don't just note
          the right answer and move on. Come back to the questions you failed
          and answer them again later until you get them right consistently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get explanations for anything you don't
          understand.</strong> If you're unsure why an answer is correct,
          find out — a memorised answer you don't understand won't survive a
          slightly reworded question on the day.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        If you've already failed once, our guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/if-you-fail/">what happens if you fail</a>
        explains the resit process. And when you're ready to apply, see
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-apply/"
          >how to apply for your licence</a
        >.
      </p>

